# Honest fio (do not reinvent)

`--direct=1` only skips the **kernel** page cache. It does **not** skip efs's
userspace dcache. A `time_based` fio with no `end_fsync` therefore reports
memory bandwidth: the old `run_tests.sh perf` suite claimed single sw-1m
9538 and 9-client 106 GiB/s, which is physically impossible (30 s at that
rate is 3.2 TiB against ~124 GB of actual storage). That suite is deleted;
if you find a write number anywhere without `end_fsync` in its command
line, it is fiction. Sanity-check every write number: bytes written ≤
on-disk `du` of `/data1/0*/efs` (× ~1.5 for 2+1 EC).

## Method (required)

- **Writes:** `--end_fsync=1`, **no** `--time_based`. Flush is in the clock.
- **Reads:** remount every participating client first (or host N reads host
  N+1's tree). Same-mount read after write is dcache.
- **Shape:** 9 jobs × 2g, `psync`, `bs` per test; 4k tests use 256m.
  50g file = **1 job**, `--filename=big50`.
- **Hosts:** 1 = 007; 4 = 007–010; 9 = 007–015. Need `.ib` on
  `run_tests.sh` host args.
- **Must be the FUSE export.** Before every job: `findmnt -o FSTYPE` on
  `/tmp/efs-mount` must be `fuse.efs-fuse`. Log line `FUSE_OK`. Treat
  `NOT_FUSE` or fio `Disk stats: md0`/`sda` as abort — that is local disk
  (dead daemon leaves a plain dir). Same class of bug as posix-on-tmpfs.
- **Harness (use this, do not rewrite):**
  `bash tests/stress/fio_honest_matrix.sh results/perf/<id>-honest`
  Current: `results/perf/20260918-w4-honest/` (1-client + morning 4-client
  writes) and `results/perf/20260918-w4-honest4/gate.txt` (8g 4/9-client dd).
  W3 8 GiB dd+fsync: `results/perf/20260918-w3-split/gate.txt` (639,
  best 724). `FIO_SKIP1=1` skips the 1-client sweep; `FIO_ONLY=1` skips 4/9.
  Prior: `results/perf/20260917-honest/` plus `results/perf/20260918-dd-1c/`.

## Current numbers (Sep 17–18, Raft+KV, 19810, TCP) — MiB/s

4- and 9-client **writes share a ceiling and get worse, not better.**
8 GiB dd+fsync own-file: 1-client **639**, 4-client **251** (0.41×),
9-client first-write **202** (0.32×). That is 3.8 % / 0.46 % / 0.37 % of
the 16.7 / 44 / 44 GB/s ceilings. Do not quote 9-client 007–010 13–31 s
(stale 4-client files). Full 4/9 fio reads and 9-client fio writes were
not finished: after two 4-client 9×2g jobs the REPORT tail exceeds the
400 s ssh budget (do not raise it). Morning 4-client sw-1m AGG **1589**
(2.3× that run's 694) is the best 4-client fio write.

| test | 1 client | 4 client AGG |
| sw-1m | 694 | 1589 |
| ow-1m | 694 | 636 |
| rw-1m | 699 | 343 |
| rw-128k | 712 | — |
| rw-4k | 385 | — |
| sr-1m | 4102 | — |
| rr-1m | 2556 | — |
| rr-128k | 1577 | — |
| rr-4k | 154 | — |
| sw-50g | 341 | — |

After WAL hold / activate-mask / pipelined propose, honest 9×2g sw-1m is
**924**. Intra-job write samples run at several GiB/s — the wall is the
`fsync`, so only the end-to-end number counts.

**Do not compare these against the pre-Raft engine's numbers** (Aug 2026:
1-client sw-1m 2355, 9-client 3599). That engine is deleted, its result
dirs are gone, and its metadata path was a whole-table RAM snapshot with
no per-op Raft commit — the two are not the same system. The write gap is
W4 (multi-client) plus the leftover REPORT pack+push tail, not a
regression to bisect.

**Shape that still holds:** reads scale with clients, writes share a
ceiling. The NVMe ceiling is 16.7–21.4 GB/s **per host**, so every write
wall measured so far is software, not media. A 50g single-job file is
slower than 9×2g on one client (less parallelism).

## Profile a single write

**ptrace is OPEN (verified Sep 1 2026 by live attach on all 15 hosts):**
`kernel.yama.ptrace_scope=0` on node9901, fstor007 and fcstor003–015, so
`strace -p`, `gdb -p`, `vgdb` and LSan all work same-uid. It is **runtime
only** (`sysctl -w`); `/etc/sysctl.d/99-ptrace.conf` still says 2, so it
reverts on reboot — ask the USER to re-apply, do not edit the conf.
**Kernel-side profiling is OPEN as of Sep 19 2026 (user ran
`sysctl -w kernel.perf_event_paranoid=1 kernel.kptr_restrict=0` via clush on
fcstor003–015, node9901, fstor007):** `perf record -a`/`-g` with kernel
symbols, `perf trace`, `perf sched`, `perf top` all work same-uid. Runtime
only (`sysctl -w`) like ptrace_scope — reverts on reboot; ask the USER to
re-apply, do not edit `/etc/sysctl.d`. `/sys/kernel/tracing/events` may
still be unreadable (that is tracefs perms, not paranoid).
Still do NOT restart daemons under strace — the 353 MiB/s
`Disk stats: md0` run was local RAID after the daemons were killed, not a
tracing overhead measurement. Attach to a RUNNING daemon instead. Same-uid:

```
perf record -e cpu-clock -g --call-graph fp -p $(pgrep -x efsd) -o /tmp/perf-efsd.data sleep 20
perf stat -p $(pgrep -x efsd) sleep 20
# same for efs-fuse; then:
timeout 25 perf report --stdio --no-children -n -i /tmp/perf-efsd.data
```

What the profiles show (Sep 17–18, and the same on the old engine): the
client's on-CPU time is blake3 in `hash_write_fragments` /
`dcache_flush_slot_inner`, the server is near-idle, and the wall is
off-CPU — `fsync`/REPORT wait, not hashing. Optimizing blake3 does not
move the number; splitting the fsync tail is the lever (W3).
